{
  "heading": "Configuration binding: Yon",
  "axis": "language",
  "summary": "One Setup role class carries the whole policy. Settings binds the YON_CATALOG_ prefix, and the Setup class's own fields are the settings, with @Min and @Max on a field. WhenProfile selects the active environment. WhenSetting turns the bean on or off based on a bound property. Setting binds a single canonical yon. path with a literal default. Its class, Endpoint, is a model in `server/models/` (ADR 0092); the Rust controller imports it with `use crate::models::{Endpoint, Settings}`. Every YON_CATALOG_ name in the snapshot must be declared. WhenProfile and WhenSetting are both decided before configure runs. The private snapshot stays private. Secrets live in .env. server/application.json holds placeholders and safe defaults. The controller returns selected fields that are not secret, never the resolved configuration.",
  "body": "Settings takes the prefix and, if you need it, \"names\":{\"pageSize\":\"PAGE_LIMIT\"}, which renames one variable. By default the name after the prefix is the field name in capital letters with underscores, so pageSize reads YON_CATALOG_PAGE_SIZE. With that option it reads YON_CATALOG_PAGE_LIMIT. Only Rust and Go use \"defaults\":{\"field\":value}, as the Go tab shows (ADR 0055).\n\nThe Lua tab binds the same Settings policy. Lua cannot write the Endpoint model yet: a Lua model that carries Setting stops the build with TY1310 (ADR 0092). So the Lua controller reads the profile from the application snapshot, `application.yon.active.profile`.\n\nSettings reads server/application.json and .env. It does not read the environment of ty itself. A handler gets an outside variable, such as HOME, only when server.environment in server/application.json lists its name (ADR 0026).\n\nC++ has no tab here, because C++ Yon code has only route controllers so far: the build refuses `Setup` in C++ with `TY1308` (ADR 0099).",
